Task 1: Boot into Multi-User, Graphical, or Rescue Mode
Subtask 1.1: Check Current Target
Open a terminal.
Run the following command to check the current target:
systemctl get-default
Expected Output:
graphical.target (default for most desktop systems) or multi-user.target (for servers).
Subtask 1.2: Boot into Multi-User Mode (No GUI)
To switch to multi-user mode (command-line only):

sudo systemctl isolate multi-user.target
Expected Outcome:
The GUI will exit, and the system will switch to a text-based login.

Verify the change:

systemctl get-default
Expected Output:
multi-user.target

Subtask 1.3: Boot into Graphical Mode
To return to the graphical mode:
sudo systemctl isolate graphical.target
Expected Outcome:
The GUI will start, and you will see the desktop environment.
Subtask 1.4: Boot into Rescue Mode (Emergency Shell)
Reboot the system and interrupt the boot process (press e in GRUB).
Append systemd.unit=rescue.target to the kernel command line.
Press Ctrl+X to boot. Expected Outcome:
The system boots into a minimal rescue shell (single-user mode).
Task 2: Understand and Configure systemd Targets
Subtask 2.1: List Available Targets
List all available targets:
systemctl list-units --type=target --all
Expected Output:
A list of targets (multi-user.target, graphical.target, rescue.target, etc.).
Subtask 2.2: Set Default Target
To set multi-user.target as the default:

sudo systemctl set-default multi-user.target
Verification:

systemctl get-default
Expected Output:
multi-user.target

Revert to graphical.target (if needed):

sudo systemctl set-default graphical.target
Task 3: Practice Switching Between Targets
Subtask 3.1: Temporarily Switch to a Different Target
Switch to multi-user.target without setting it as default:

sudo systemctl isolate multi-user.target
Expected Outcome:
The system switches to CLI mode.

Switch back to graphical.target:

sudo systemctl isolate graphical.target
Subtask 3.2: Reboot into a Specific Target
To reboot directly into rescue.target:
sudo systemctl reboot --boot-loader-entry=rescue.target
Expected Outcome:
The system reboots into rescue mode.
